Subject: format with metadata checksums

mkfs stripped EXT4_FRO_COM_METADATA_CSUM behind a "handle this some day"
comment, so every volume it produced was a 2010 filesystem: no CRC between a
flipped bit in a group descriptor and silently wrong accounting. mke2fs has
enabled the feature by default for years, and the runtime side of this tree
already computes and verifies every checksum ext4 defines -- the same code
that maintains a mke2fs-made volume maintains one of ours.

Turning it on at format time needed less than the comment feared, because
most of what mkfs writes already passes through that runtime code before the
format returns: init_bgs() re-initialises every bitmap through
ext4_fs_init_block_bitmap/inode_bitmap (which set the bitmap checksums),
rewrites every primary group descriptor through
ext4_fs_put_block_group_ref (descriptor checksum), and the inodes and
directories are built by the normal allocation and dirent code (inode,
dirent-tail, htree and extent checksums). Three gaps were real:

  * the superblock copies: write_sblocks() bypasses ext4_sb_write(), and
    block_group_index -- stamped per copy -- is inside the checksummed
    range. Each copy now checksums itself after the stamp. This is also an
    ordering requirement, not just correctness: ext4_mkfs() re-opens the
    filesystem mid-format through ext4_fs_init(), which verifies the
    primary superblock's checksum and would refuse its own half-made volume.
  * the backup group descriptor tables: write_bgroup_block() copies the raw
    descriptor buffer into every backup group, and nothing later repairs
    those copies -- init_bgs() only ever reaches the primary table. The
    descriptors are checksummed at build time now, so every copy inherits
    a valid checksum; the primaries are recomputed again afterwards.
  * the checksum seed: s_checksum_seed is frozen into the superblock and
    metadata_csum_seed set, as mke2fs does by default -- it is what lets
    tune2fs -U change the UUID later without rewriting every checksum.

GDT_CSUM stays stripped: ext4 treats it and metadata_csum as mutually
exclusive. ext4_sb_set_csum() and ext4_fs_bg_checksum() lose their static
for this; neither needs more than the superblock and a descriptor.

Verified by the format suite's geometry sweep -- e2fsck, which now verifies
every checksum on every combination of thirteen sizes, three block sizes and
three generations -- plus the Linux-kernel round trip, and the full
reorder/orphan/write suites unchanged.
--- a/src/ext4_mkfs.c
+++ b/src/ext4_mkfs.c
@@ -278,6 +278,17 @@
 
 	memcpy(sb->uuid, info->uuid, UUID_SIZE);
 
+	/* Freeze the checksum seed into the superblock, as mke2fs does by
+	 * default: metadata_csum_seed is what lets tune2fs -U change the UUID
+	 * later without rewriting every checksum on the volume. The formula
+	 * matches ext4_sb_csum_seed()'s fallback, so the two paths agree. */
+	if (info->feat_ro_compat & EXT4_FRO_COM_METADATA_CSUM) {
+		sb->features_incompatible |=
+			to_le32(EXT4_FINCOM_BG_USE_META_CSUM);
+		sb->checksum_seed = to_le32(ext4_crc32c(EXT4_CRC32_INIT,
+							sb->uuid, UUID_SIZE));
+	}
+
